# 20. FINAL DESIGN OBJECTIVE

OmniLens should be recognizable as a premium AI accessibility product: visually confident, technically credible, approachable and genuinely designed around the people it serves.

The public website should make the mission understandable within seconds. The application should make the next action obvious. The voice controller should let the user switch tasks naturally. The visual system should remain attractive without sacrificing contrast, readability or control.

**The ultimate design principle is simple:**

“Beautiful enough to capture the world's attention. Accessible enough to put the user first. Reliable enough to earn their trust.”

The final implementation must be validated against the actual React application, backend capabilities, browser support and accessibility testing results. Any feature that is not yet implemented should be clearly identified as planned rather than presented as working.

## My strongest recommendation, bro ❤️

Make the public landing page visually stunning, but keep the actual assistant screen calm, spacious and extremely easy to operate. Those two experiences should share the same branding without forcing blind users to navigate a marketing-heavy interface.

The three things that will make OmniLens stand out are:

1. A recognizable visual identity — midnight navy, warm ivory and accessible teal.
2. A truly voice-first interaction — the user can say “Describe,” then “Read text,” then “Find bottle” without navigating back through menus.
3. Real accessibility, not just accessibility-themed styling — screen-reader support, keyboard operation, high contrast, clear spoken status and reliable Stop/Cancel behaviour.

That is the direction I would use for OmniLens: a beautiful global-facing product with a genuinely user-first assistant at its heart.